IMO, it's not really quirkier than the possibility of finding
another role's quest artifact in a bones pile.

> I agree with David that quest-artifact-wishing is sort
> of cheesy. I don't know what you guys think about the difference
> between quest-artifact-wishing and ordinary artifact-weapon-wishing.

Well, quest artifacts are strictly limited to co-aligned characters.
That limits the cheesiness somewhat.  You cannot, for example,
wish for both the Eye and the Master Key and use them both at
the same time.  (With a helm of opposite alignment, I suppose
you could potentially do both _in the same game_, but it would
be weird and largely pointless and have other drawbacks.)

This may be the reason non-quest artifacts are generally either
rather limited in power or have major drawbacks of their own
(*cough* Stormbringer).  Because any character can obtain
them, if there were too many powerful ones, it would create
a much larger degree of cheesiness.  Grayswandir, I have
been recently informed, is a notable exception.  Perhaps in
a future version Grayswandir should be converted into a
quest artifact for some new role, since it is so powerful.

> Myself I never wished for artifacts in general exactly for
> that reason, nonetheless I also do not consider it a real
> conduct; for me it just feels wrong doing it. YMMV.

I kind of feel that way about credit cloning and other
forms of dishonest shop exploitation.  I don't mind artifact
wishing at all, because wishes are an inherently scarce
resource and must be carefully rationed (unless you're,
like, doing scripted farming or some such, and artifact
wishing can't even compete with that for cheesiness).
But exploiting the shopkeepers' inability to tell when
your pet's behavior is clearly something that you have
deliberately encouraged just feels wrong to me.  I've
done it a couple of times, but I don't like it and, as a
rule, I generally don't do it.  It feels like cheating.

Honestly, I wouldn't mind playing a variant wherein
shopkeepers stand in the doorway whenever any
monster (not just the player) is carrying anything
they haven't paid for and refuse to let the monster
out of the shop until it drops whatever.  This could
create difficulties with pets that permanently equip
themselves (e.g., Centaurs), but most of the ones
that are powerful enough to get into shops if you
lock the door are also powerful enough to kill the
shopkeeper, so.


